export anchor PluginDependencies:
    description:
        A plugin can build on another. The test plugin launches agentic tools
        with the fluency prompt, and it's the Belay plugin that knows the
        tools, how each is launched, and what the prompt says. So the test
        plugin requires Belay, and calls what Belay exports.

        ```piton
        \\\
        piton-plugin TestPlugin:
            name: test
            requires: [belay]
        \\\
        ```

        ```typescript
        \\\
        // In Belay:
        export default definePlugin({
          exports: { agents: (ctx) => ({ list, launch, fluency }) },
        });

        // In the test plugin:
        const agents = ctx.require("belay").agents;
        \\\
        ```

    loading:
        A plugin's requirements are loaded with it, into the same runtime,
        before it runs. Each plugin's modules are its own, and a plugin
        imports only @piton/plugin and its own files; another plugin is
        reached only through ctx.require, by a name its declaration lists.

        A requirement the project doesn't have, a requirement on itself, and
        requirements that go round in a circle are errors in the declaration
        that requires them, and that plugin is not loaded.

    exports:
        definePlugin takes exports beside commands and build: named values
        and functions other plugins can use. A function exported as a factory
        is given the exporting plugin's own context, so it runs with that
        plugin's capabilities, never the caller's, and a caller can't use it
        to do what its own declaration doesn't allow, beyond what the
        exporting plugin may.

    commands:
        Requiring a plugin gives access to its exports only. Its commands and
        its build are its own, and run once however many plugins require it.
